window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][62] = "<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-deliberate-practice\">deliberate practice</a> is from C34, <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a> from C15 and <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliberate practice</strong> (<code>MM-deliberate-practice</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-deliberate-practice\">deliberate practice</a>, C34). Practice that targets a specific weakness with immediate feedback improves performance; repetition without feedback does not. The plan's drills, outside scorers and error log are this model made routine.</p>\n<p><strong>The Pareto principle</strong> (<code>MM-pareto</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a>, C15). A few dimensions and gaps decide most of the loop outcome; importance times gap finds them.</p>\n<p><strong>Spacing</strong> (<code>MM-spacing</code>). Practice spread over time is generally retained better than the same practice crammed together, and the best gap between sessions grows with how long you need to remember <code>[V: Cepeda et al., Psychological Bulletin 132(3), 354-380, 2006, a meta-analysis of spaced versus massed practice]</code>. Retests at a day, a week and a month use it.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). Ask how the plan would fail: no baseline, no outside scoring, applying too early, no fallback, a first-90-days plan with no shipped result. Install a check for each.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<p>What your future partners expect from a new APM in the first 90 days, which is also what you can practice now.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineers</strong> expect a clear problem, honest dates and context on users; they notice quickly whether you ask good questions and keep promises about scope. In your first month, ask each engineer what a PM does that wastes their time.</li>\n<li><strong>Designers and researchers</strong> expect you to bring the user problem and evidence, not a solution; share your ten user conversations with them and ask to join their sessions.</li>\n<li><strong>Data scientists</strong> expect a precise question and agreed metrics; run your first funnel query with them and define the metric together. Your sandbox practice shows here.</li>\n<li><strong>Your manager</strong> expects the answer first, bad news early and a weekly update (C32); the written 30-60-90 agreement and the check-in are how you make that routine.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c37-checklist-before-each-month-and-before-each-loop\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before each month and before each loop</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Do I have a current baseline and a re-score date?</li>\n<li>Are my drill hours allocated by importance times gap?</li>\n<li>Have I had an outside-scored mock this week?</li>\n<li>Is my calibration (bias and error) known and shrinking?</li>\n<li>Do I have a story with numbers from each completed project?</li>\n<li>Are my company briefs dated and sourced from primary documents?</li>\n<li>Has the readiness gate been re-tested before this application?</li>\n<li>Is my fallback plan written?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c37-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c37-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C37-01 · The top choice in month two · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A candidate with about two years of product experience starts the plan in October. Excited by a posting for their top-choice company, and unsure whether another chance will come, they apply in the second week and, after a quick rehearsal, pass the recruiter screen.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> No baseline yet beyond one self-scored mock; a fixed date for the first round in three weeks; a top choice that the candidate has said they may not get another chance at; no understanding of how reapplication works.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The first loop has four rounds. Outside scores from a practice partner who watched a recording of the candidate's rounds afterward (invented): product design 3.5, analytics 2.5, execution 3.0, behavioral 3.5. The loop ends after the analytics round with a polite rejection. The candidate's own self-scores had been 4.0, 3.5, 3.5 and 4.0 (a bias of about +0.6). The recruiter says reapplication is possible after 12 months (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The candidate used their top-choice application before they had a baseline, a calibrated sense of their level or a readiness gate, and lost the cycle at a dimension they had not measured.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The baseline would have shown analytics as the weakest dimension (2.5 against a gate of 4.0) and a bias of +0.6, both visible in the first month with two outside-scored mocks. The plan's waves (C37.2) and gate (C37.7) exist for exactly this: top targets are for after the gate passes, and lower-stakes applications provide practice. The cost of the early application is a 12-month wait (in this scenario), nearly the whole plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep applying to top choices as postings appear</td>\n<td>Perceived momentum</td>\n<td>Burns the best targets before readiness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Run the baseline, apply first to wave 0 roles, use the gate for top choices, and plan the next cycle for the top choice</td>\n<td>Readiness and learning</td>\n<td>Slower start; a missed window for the top choice this cycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Stop applying and prepare for twelve months</td>\n<td>Safety</td>\n<td>No real-world feedback</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a cycle on the top choice and protects it for the next; A feels like momentum and spent the chance; C removes learning from real loops.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B for the future: withdraw nothing now, treat the first loop as data, write the gate before the next application, and start wave 0 roles for practice while drilling analytics; ask each recruiter about reapplication rules before applying.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, the candidate's analytics average rose from 2.5 to 3.9 over six months with 18 outside-scored mocks; the gate passed in month ten; they applied again when the top-choice window reopened and reached the final round (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the early loop had succeeded, the lesson is still that luck is not a plan; many candidates with a baseline and a gate would have the same offer with a lower risk. The lesson: measure first, keep top targets for after the gate, and use lower-stakes loops to learn.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Eight questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), and every figure not taken from a cited public source is invented (invented). Each ends with follow-ups and answers. No company tag is attached because the topic is not tied to a single company.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-01 · Beginner · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you keep up with this industry?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I read tech news and follow people on social media.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names primary sources and a routine.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Describes a weekly routine with primary sources, dated facts, an application step and a specific recent example, and says how it separates fact from inference.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I use a one-hour weekly routine built on primary sources. Twenty minutes scanning the official newsrooms of a few companies I follow, such as Google's Keyword blog and Meta's newsroom; twenty-five minutes reading one primary document in depth, such as a shareholder letter or a filing section; and fifteen minutes applying it: who a launch is for, what I would measure, one risk and one question. For example, Netflix's shareholder letter for the third quarter of 2023 said that membership in its ad-supported plan was up almost 70% over the previous quarter and that 30% of sign-ups in markets with the plan chose it <code>[V: shareholder letter, October 2023]</code>. What I took from it was that relative growth tells me nothing without the base and that I'd want to know whether those sign-ups were new demand or customers moving down from a higher-priced plan.</p>\n<p>&quot;I keep each fact with its source and date in a note, and I label anything I can't source as my inference, so that I don't present a guess as a fact.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Primary sources; a routine; a specific example; fact versus inference.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you do about news that comes from commentary and not the company? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I treat it as a lead: I look for the primary document that supports it before I rely on it, and if I can't find one I call it a rumor or an inference when I talk about it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you decide which companies to follow? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By my targets and the products I use, so that I can compare what the company says with my own experience. I keep it to two or three so the habit stays at an hour.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-02 · Beginner · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What would you do in your first 30 days as an associate product manager?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Meet the team and learn the product.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names what to learn: users, metrics, partners, decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A concrete plan with deliverables, a written agreement with the manager, and a one-page output by day 30; says what it would not do.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd aim to learn how decisions get made and what the numbers say, not to change anything yet. In the first week I'd agree with my manager what success looks like at 30, 60 and 90 days, in writing, and set a weekly 30-minute check-in. I'd use the product as a user for several hours, read the support tickets and the last two quarters of launch reviews, and meet each partner for 30 minutes with the same questions: what do you need from a product manager, what has gone well and badly, and what should I know that isn't written down. I'd map the team's decision rights and the metrics and how each is defined, and run one funnel query with the data scientist.</p>\n<p>&quot;By day 30 I'd share a one-page summary of what I learned and what I still don't understand. I wouldn't propose a large change before I understand the history: a good early contribution is a small thing I own, such as a metric definition or a weekly report.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A concrete plan; the manager agreement; a day-30 output; restraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if your manager gives no direction? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd write my own 30-60-90 plan, share it and ask for corrections. Most managers prefer a draft to an open question, and it shows initiative.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is the biggest mistake new associates make? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Trying to prove themselves with a big idea before they understand the context. The small shipped win with a measure builds more credibility.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-03 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How did you prepare for this interview, and what did you learn about yourself?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I practiced a lot and I feel ready.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A baseline, a plan by gaps and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Outside scoring, a calibration finding, a written gate, a number for improvement, and a self-knowledge insight that changed a habit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I started with a baseline: three recorded mocks scored by an outside scorer, which showed analytics as my weakest area at 2.9 against 3.8 on product sense. I allocated drill hours by importance times gap, so the biggest share went to analytics, and re-scored every two months. After six mocks I computed my bias: my self-scores ran about 0.6 above the outside scorer's, so I corrected for it and used outside scores for decisions. By month ten my analytics average was 3.9 and my bias was under half a point (invented).</p>\n<p>&quot;What I learned about myself is that I overrate how I sound; the recordings and the outside scores corrected that. I changed one habit as a result: I score before I hear anyone's feedback. I'd bring the same loop to the job: a baseline, outside feedback and small shipped work.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Baseline; gap-driven plan; calibration; evidence of change; a changed habit.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What was hardest? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Practicing the dimension I was weak at instead of the ones I was good at; it felt worse. I used the numbers to keep honest: the allocation came from the table, not my mood.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How did you know you were ready to apply? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I wrote a gate in advance: averages of at least 4.0 on high-weight dimensions, calibration within half a point and three full mock loops with an independent lean of hire or better. When it said hold, I held for four weeks.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-04 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Tell me about a time you taught yourself something quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I learned SQL from videos.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A goal, a method and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A baseline, a practice designed around feedback, a result with a number, honesty about what was a sandbox, and how the skill was used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>(A composite example; figures are invented.)</em> &quot;I couldn't answer my own product questions without asking an analyst, which took two days. I decided to learn SQL well enough to answer funnel, retention and segment questions myself. I built a practice sandbox: a small synthetic dataset in SQLite, and I wrote three queries a week, each with a stated question, the output, one caveat and one decision it would inform. I asked the data scientist on my team to review one query a week.</p>\n<p>&quot;In six weeks my turnaround for a basic funnel question dropped from two days to under an hour, and in week five I found that one channel brought 35% of our signups with the lowest activation, which changed a test we were planning. I'm clear that the sandbox was practice; the real analysis used our data after the data scientist confirmed my definitions. I still ask for a review on anything that will drive a decision.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A baseline; a designed practice; a result with a number; honesty about practice versus real work.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What went wrong? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;My first retention query counted a user multiple times because I joined without de-duplicating; the data scientist caught it. Since then I check the count of users against a known total before reading any percentage.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How did you decide it was enough? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;When I could answer the three standard questions (funnel, cohort, segment) without help and explain the caveats; beyond that I rely on the data scientist for anything complex.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-05 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> There is far too much to learn for this role. How do you decide what to learn first?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The most important things first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses importance and gaps.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures the gaps with outside scoring, weights by importance, allocates time proportionally, sets numeric targets, re-measures and reallocates, and keeps a minimum on strengths.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I measure before I choose. I take outside-scored practice results by competency, weight each by how much it matters for the role, and multiply by the gap to a target level: importance times gap. Then I allocate time in proportion. For example, with analytics at 2.9, AI at 2.6 and problem solving at 4.0, and weights of 3, 2 and 3, the products are 6.3, 4.8 and 3.0, so analytics and AI get most of the hours and problem solving a smaller maintenance share. I set a numeric target and a re-score date for the top two, and reallocate when the gaps change. I keep a minimum on strengths, because they decay without practice.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Measure then weight; proportional allocation; targets and re-scores.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the most important thing isn't measurable? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I define an observable proxy, such as a recorded answer scored against written anchors, or feedback from a partner, and I say it's a proxy. An imperfect measure beats a feeling.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if priorities change, such as a new target company? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I re-weight importance for that company's loop (C33), re-run the allocation and adjust the hours; the structure stays the same.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-06 · Intermediate · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What would you ship in your first 90 days, and how would you know it worked?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A small feature.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A small change with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A small, low-risk change chosen from evidence, a success criterion and review date written before the build, a staged release, and a retrospective.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd pick a small, low-risk change chosen from what I learned in the first 60 days, for example the step with the largest drop in the onboarding funnel. Say it's a clearer explanation on a permissions step, where 38% of new users drop. I'd write the success criterion before building: completion up by at least three points, with support contacts flat as a guardrail, reviewed four weeks after launch. I'd release to 10% of users first with a rollback trigger (C17).</p>\n<p>&quot;At day 90 I'd review the result against the criterion and write a one-page retrospective: what the metric did, what I'd change, and the plan for the next quarter, agreed with my manager. If it didn't move, I'd say so and say what I learned; a small measured change that failed still builds credibility.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence-chosen change; criterion first; staged release; a retrospective.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if engineering can't fit it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd shrink it: a copy change or configuration might fit, or move it to the next cycle and ship something even smaller. The goal at 90 days is one small result with a measure, not the biggest idea.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the metric does not move? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd check whether it reached the users who need it and whether the measurement was sound, then decide to iterate once or stop; I'd tell my manager early and record the learning.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-07 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Three weeks into the job your manager is very busy and gives you no direction. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd wait for direction,&quot; or &quot;I'd start building something.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Proposes a plan and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Writes a 30-60-90 draft, makes asking cheap for the manager, uses peers and documents to learn, delivers a small win, and keeps the manager informed on a fixed rhythm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd make it easy for my manager to give direction. I'd write a draft 30-60-90 plan with what I intend to learn, own and ship, send it with three specific questions, and ask for a 20-minute slot to correct it. Meanwhile I'd learn from other sources: peer product managers, the launch reviews and metrics, the partners' one-to-ones. I'd pick one small thing to own and deliver it, so that I'm useful while direction forms. I'd send a four-line weekly update (done, next, blockers, one ask) so my manager can see progress with a minute's reading, and say plainly when I need a decision.</p>\n<p>&quot;If after another two weeks there's still no direction, I'd name it directly and kindly: 'I've been working from this plan; is this the right priority, or should I change it?' A manager who is busy usually responds well to a draft they can correct.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A draft plan; low-cost asks; learning from other sources; a small win; a weekly update.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the manager's silence continues for weeks? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask a skip-level or a trusted peer how the team handles direction, and keep delivering visible, small work. I'd also consider whether I'm asking in the right form: a decision with options is easier to answer than an open question.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you disagree with the priorities you were given? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd bring evidence and a proposal, not a complaint, ask for a decision, and then commit if the decision stands, noting the signal that would make me raise it again (C32.6).&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c37-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C37-08 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> After 90 days, how will you know whether you are performing at the level expected of an associate product manager, and what if you are not?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;My manager will tell me.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names indicators and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines indicators with the manager at the start, checks them at 30, 60 and 90 days with partner feedback, compares with evidence (a shipped change, reliable updates), diagnoses gaps early and makes a plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd agree indicators with my manager at the start, in writing, so that the standard is shared: for example, that I can explain the product's users, metrics and decisions; that I have shipped one small change with a measure; that partners find me reliable and clear; and that my updates are on time. I'd check them myself at 30, 60 and 90 days against evidence, and ask for feedback at day 45 from my manager and two partners: one thing to keep and one to change.</p>\n<p>&quot;If I'm behind on one, I'd say so early and make a specific plan: if I haven't shipped, I'd shrink the work; if a partner finds me unreliable, I'd look at dates and scope promises I've made and fix the process. I'd also check my own calibration: if my read of how I'm doing differs from my manager's by more than a little, I'd ask for examples. The aim is to find out at day 45, not at the review.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Agreed indicators; evidence; early feedback; a plan; calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you disagree with your manager's assessment? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask for specific examples and the standard they're using, share my evidence, and look for where we see different things. If we still differ, I'd ask what would change their view and make that my goal for the next month.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle imposter feelings? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I treat them as a signal to look at evidence: a log of what I've shipped and learned, and feedback from partners. I also ask peers how they started; most people recognize the feeling, and it fades as the evidence builds.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c37-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are invented unless stated.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-01 · A baseline allocation</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Outside-scored averages (1 to 5): product sense 3.5, discovery 2.8, problem solving 3.2, analytics 3.4, execution 3.0, technical 3.6, AI 2.4, design and UX 3.9, strategy 3.3, behavioral 3.0. Importance: 3 for product sense, problem solving, analytics, execution and behavioral; 2 for the others. Allocate 90 drill hours and name the top two targets.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The baseline (FW-C37-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C37-01 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Importance × gap (gap = 5 − score): product sense 3 × 1.5 = 4.5; discovery 2 × 2.2 = 4.4; problem solving 3 × 1.8 = 5.4; analytics 3 × 1.6 = 4.8; execution 3 × 2.0 = 6.0; technical 2 × 1.4 = 2.8; AI 2 × 2.6 = 5.2; design and UX 2 × 1.1 = 2.2; strategy 2 × 1.7 = 3.4; behavioral 3 × 2.0 = 6.0. The sum is 44.7. Hours (90 × each ÷ 44.7): product sense 9.1; discovery 8.9; problem solving 10.9; analytics 9.7; execution 12.1; technical 5.6; AI 10.5; design and UX 4.4; strategy 6.8; behavioral 12.1. The top two targets are execution and behavioral (12.1 hours each); problem solving (10.9) is third and AI (10.5) fourth; AI is the weakest raw score, so set a target for it too (2.4 to 3.2). Set numeric targets and a re-score date for each.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Set a floor of 4 hours for every competency and scale the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A formula guides and judgment adjusts for the company's weighting (C33).</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-02 · Budget your own year</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You have 10 hours a week for 40 weeks. Allocate the hours in the plan's proportions and compare the reading budget with the 105.9 study hours of the full reading map. What do you cut?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The twelve-month plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Proportions 2.0 : 2.5 : 1.5 : 1.5 : 1.0 : 0.5 (of 9).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Total 400 hours. Reading and worked examples: 400 × 2 ÷ 9 = 88.9; drills 111.1; mocks 66.7; projects 66.7; literacy 44.4; journal 22.2. The reading budget (88.9) is 17.0 hours short of the full map (105.9). Cut by need, in this order: first the reference library (12.4 hours) except what your gaps require, then the deep dives that do not match your targets (for example C19 or C20 if your targets are consumer roles). Do not cut mocks or timed drills.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Take the shortfall from rereading time you would have spent anyway, which retrieval practice replaces.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A shorter plan covers less; the loss is acceptable if the high-weight dimensions are covered.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-03 · Evaluate the gate</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Last six outside-scored mocks (means): product sense 4.1, problem solving 4.3, analytics 4.2, execution 4.0, behavioral 4.4; discovery 3.6, technical 3.4, AI 3.6, design and UX 3.9, strategy 3.5. Bias +0.4, error 0.7. Three full mock loops: leans of lean hire, lean hire, lean no hire. Apply the gate (C37.7) and say what you do.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The readiness gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C37-06 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) High-weight dimensions are all at least 4.0 (execution is exactly 4.0): pass; others need at least 3.5: technical is 3.4: fail by 0.1. (2) Calibration: bias 0.4 (within ±0.5) and error 0.7 (at most 0.75): pass. (3) Loops: two of three at lean hire or better: pass. Result: hold on one condition. Plan: two weeks of technical fluency drills (C21) with two more outside-scored mocks that include a technical question, then re-test; apply to wave 1 roles meanwhile. The margin is small, so avoid treating a 0.1 shortfall as a reason for a long delay: re-test quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Treat the condition as a &quot;pass with watch&quot; for the first loop if the target company's loop has little technical weight (C33), and say why in writing before you apply.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A strict gate protects top targets; a tiny shortfall should cost days, not months.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-04 · A story-project card</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fill in the story-project card (FW-C37-04) for &quot;a metrics audit of the product I work on,&quot; with the decision it forces, the artifact, the numbers to capture, collaborators, time box and the story it yields.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Projects that become stories.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C37-04 and C37.4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Decision it forces:</em> which metric the team treats as its headline and what its guardrail is. <em>Artifact:</em> a one-page metric card (definition, owner, data source, guardrail) for five metrics, plus a finding. <em>Numbers to capture before starting:</em> the current value and trend of each metric; the share of decisions in the last quarter that cited each; for the finding, the gap (for example, the headline rose 9% while four-week retention was flat). <em>Collaborators:</em> the data scientist (definitions) and the engineering lead (instrumentation); credit them. <em>Time box:</em> 1.5 hours a week for four weeks. <em>Story:</em> &quot;I audited our five core metrics, found that our headline measure counted accidental sessions, proposed a replacement with a guardrail, and we changed our weekly report&quot; (C05, C29), written in the C31 anatomy once the result is known, stating which numbers are real.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose the project whose decision your manager already cares about, so that the result is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A narrow audit yields a credible story sooner than a complete one.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-05 · Read a channel table</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A synthetic dataset gives: channel A, 3,000 signups, 1,500 activated, 450 returned; channel B, 2,000 signups, 600 activated, 120 returned. Write the SQL idea for the activation and return rates by channel, compute them, read them and state one caveat and one decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The analytics sandbox.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Idea:</em> join users to de-duplicated sets of activated users and returned users, group by channel, and divide the counts by signups (a LEFT JOIN on a distinct-user subquery for each event so that each user is counted once). <em>Compute:</em> A: activation 1,500 ÷ 3,000 = 50.0%, return 450 ÷ 3,000 = 15.0%; B: activation 600 ÷ 2,000 = 30.0%, return 120 ÷ 2,000 = 6.0%. <em>Read:</em> A converts better at both steps; overall activation is (1,500 + 600) ÷ 5,000 = 42.0% and return (450 + 120) ÷ 5,000 = 11.4%, a blend that hides the gap. <em>Caveat:</em> check the definition of &quot;returned&quot; and the time window, and that each user is counted once. <em>Decision:</em> investigate why B does not activate (onboarding or targeting) before spending more on B, with a guardrail on total signups.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add the purchase rate by channel and the cost per purchaser using invented acquisition costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A synthetic example teaches the reading; real data needs definitions checked with the data scientist.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-06 · One literacy session</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Run the 60-minute routine (FW-C37-05) on one target company. Deliver three dated facts with sources, five lines of &quot;who, what to measure, one risk, one question,&quot; and one change to your company brief.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The weekly literacy habit.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Primary sources from C37.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Sixty minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C37-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A good deliverable: three facts, each with the source type, date and a link or title (for example, a shareholder letter's statement that an ad plan's membership rose almost 70% in a quarter, October 2023); five lines: <em>who it is for</em> (price-sensitive households), <em>what to measure</em> (net additions, mix of new versus downgraded sign-ups, revenue per member), <em>one risk</em> (cannibalization of the higher-priced plan), <em>one question</em> (what share of ad-plan members are new to the service), and an update to the brief (a dated line on the plan and its stated metrics). Label anything not in a source as your inference.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Do it on a company whose letter or filing you have never read, to practice finding the right section quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> One company deeply beats five superficially.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-07 · A 30-60-90 plan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You join a business-to-business product team that owns an admin console for an enterprise tool. Write a 30-60-90 plan (FW-C37-07) with goals, deliverables and evidence for each period, including one small shipped change with a success criterion and a review date.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The first job.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ten minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C37-07 and Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Days 1 to 30:</em> use the console as an administrator on a test account; read the last two quarters of support tickets and launch reviews; map decision rights and partners (including security and customer success); learn the five team metrics and one query; one-to-ones with partners; written plan agreed with the manager; a one-page summary at day 30. <em>Days 31 to 60:</em> own the weekly admin-usage report; ten calls with administrators, including two at large customers; a decision document proposing a fix for the most-cited support issue; feedback request at day 45. <em>Days 61 to 90:</em> ship one small change (for example, a bulk user-import error message that says what to fix), with the criterion written first (support tickets about import down by 25% in four weeks, with import completion flat or up) and the review at day 90, released to 10% of accounts first; a retrospective and a plan for the next quarter. Evidence: the summary, the calls log, the accepted document, the shipped change and its result.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Shadow a customer success manager for a week to learn the administrator's day.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A small change in an enterprise setting may need more review; plan the security and customer-communication steps early.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c37-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C37-08 · Portfolio math</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Suppose each loop has a 15% chance of an offer (an assumption). Compute the probability of at least one offer after 1, 3, 6, 10 and 15 loops, and the number of loops needed for about 90%. What does it mean for the plan?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The fallback plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> P(at least one) = 1 − (1 − p)^n.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C37-08 and Worked example 6.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> After 1 loop: 15%; 3 loops: 38.6%; 6: 62.3%; 10: 80.3%; 15: 91.3%. For 90%: n = ln(0.1) ÷ ln(0.85) = about 14.2, so about 15 loops. Meaning: with a per-loop chance this low, even a strong candidate should expect several loops before an offer; the plan's job is to raise p (through the gate) and to raise n (through a wide, fit-checked portfolio), and not to read three rejections as a verdict. The assumption of independence and a fixed p is rough; the point is the shape.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute at p = 25%: 3 loops 57.8%, 6 loops 82.2%; the number needed for 90% is about 8.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> More applications cost effort and raise the chance; raising p costs preparation and also raises it.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: baseline and plan.</em> Use IQ-C37-01, IQ-C37-03, IQ-C37-05, EX-C37-01, EX-C37-02 and EX-C37-04. Record three mocks, get them scored, and compute your own allocation and targets.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: gate, literacy and the first job.</em> Use IQ-C37-02, IQ-C37-04, IQ-C37-06 to IQ-C37-08, EX-C37-03, EX-C37-05 to EX-C37-08. Run one literacy session, write your gate and your fallback, and rehearse the two-minute preparation answer aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;How do you know you improved?&quot; and &quot;What was your weakest area?&quot; at random, and to ask for the source and date of every company fact you state.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I start from a measured baseline with a number?</li>\n<li>Did I allocate hours by importance times gap?</li>\n<li>Did I use outside scoring and know my calibration?</li>\n<li>Did I give a before-and-after number?</li>\n<li>Did I use primary, dated sources for company facts?</li>\n<li>Did I name a written gate and a fallback?</li>\n<li>Did my first-90-days answer include a shipped result with a criterion written first?</li>\n<li>Did I say what I learned about myself and the habit it changed?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Record the date, the prompt, the number I could not produce, my score and the one fix; re-score monthly against the checkpoint targets.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Start the plan this week.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Set up the toolkit (C37.6): a recording method, five notes files and the sandbox. Decide your weekly hours and your start and end dates. Check this week's postings against the fit check (C01.5) and decide your October-window question.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Record three mocks of different round types and get an outside scorer (C34). Compute the baseline table and the allocation (FW-C37-01). Draft your story-bank list of ten episodes and pick your first project (FW-C37-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run the first literacy session (FW-C37-05) and write the first company brief. Write the readiness gate and the fallback plan on one page, dated, before you have any results.</p>\n<p><strong>Month 1 and after.</strong> Follow the monthly table in C37.2; re-score at each checkpoint; test the gate before every top-target application. If you get an offer, switch to the 30-60-90 plan (FW-C37-07) and keep the same loop: baseline, outside feedback and small shipped work.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become assets:</strong> the baseline and allocation, the dated gate and fallback, six project stories with numbers, the dated company briefs, the sandbox queries and outputs, and the weekly journal. Keep every claim about your experience true and dated.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c37-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Start from a measured baseline: outside-scored mocks on the C34 scale, averaged by competency, with importance times gap setting the hours and numeric targets set for the top two gaps.</li>\n<li>Plan the year in months: about nine hours a week for 48 weeks (432 hours) split into study, timed drills, partner mocks, projects, literacy and review; follow the reading map (a core path of 21 chapters, eight deep dives and a five-chapter reference library, or the 80-hour fast track), re-score at five checkpoints and apply in waves.</li>\n<li>Practice by producing answers with outside scoring, space the retests, fix one error at a time, and build projects into stories with numbers that are true.</li>\n<li>Decide when to apply by a written readiness gate (scores, calibration, full loops, stories, briefs, logistics, fallback), hold top targets until it passes, and use portfolio math and a diagnosis, not a verdict, when offers do not come.</li>\n<li>If you get the offer, run the same loop at work: learn the product and system in 30 days, own a small thing and talk to users by day 60, ship one small change with a criterion written first by day 90, and write down what you learned.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c37-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The official company pages named in C37.5, read as primary sources and dated <code>[V: company pages named, checked as sources in October 2026]</code>.</li>\n<li>Roediger and Karpicke (2006) on retrieval practice, the evidence behind CS-C34-02 <code>[V: Roediger and Karpicke (2006)]</code>.</li>\n<li>Cepeda, Pashler, Vul, Wixted and Rohrer, &quot;Distributed Practice in Verbal Recall Tasks: A Review and Quantitative Synthesis,&quot; <em>Psychological Bulletin</em> 132(3), 354 to 380 (2006), a meta-analysis of the spacing evidence for verbal recall <code>[V: Psychological Bulletin 132(3), 354 to 380, May 2006, doi 10.1037/0033-2909.132.3.354]</code>.</li>\n<li>Chapters C01 (the role and the book), C31 (stories), C33 (loops and company lenses), C34 (mocks, scoring and calibration) and C16 and C32 (the first job) of this book.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reserved for the Second Edition.</strong> C35 Resume, Portfolio and Applications; C36 Offers and Negotiation; C38 Landing and Thriving: Your First Year as an APM. These numbers are reserved for the Second Edition, so the numbering jumps from C34 to C37 on purpose.</p>\n<span id=\"glossary\"></span>\n";
